{;; kmet's RFC 0014 provider lib (see README.md in this dir): the JDK
 ;; classes the jolt ecosystem does not supply that kmet's crypto/oauth/ADC
 ;; paths need — RSA (io.github.jolt-lang/crypto is EC-only by design),
 ;; java.util.Base64 MIME decoding, java.net.http.HttpTimeoutException.
 ;;
 ;; The native libs (libcrypto/libssl) and the EC/symmetric java.* classes
 ;; come from io.github.jolt-lang/crypto, which jolt.kmet.providers REQUIRES
 ;; as its first form — the load order is structural: jolt.crypto's
 ;; deps.edn :jolt/native loads the OpenSSL libraries its FFI bindings (and
 ;; ours) resolve against at load, and its install! registers the classes
 ;; this ns extends. jolt dedupes natives by :name, so no redeclaration here.
 ;;
 ;; Inert on bb/JVM: nothing in the bb classpath ever requires a jolt.kmet.*
 ;; namespace (kmet's guarded requires check for *jolt-version* first).
 :jolt/min-version "0.8.0"

 :paths ["src"]

 ;; The classes this lib installs (RFC 0014). jolt autoloads the install
 ;; namespace when a DEPENDENT namespace references one of these while it is
 ;; unregistered — the deterministic answer to the load-order trap this
 ;; setup exists to defuse: jolt.crypto's install! registers EC-only
 ;; Signature/KeyPairGenerator/KeyFactory as a side effect of ITS autoload
 ;; (javax.crypto.Mac/Cipher), and once registered a class never triggers a
 ;; provider lookup again. Claiming the classes here makes our install ns —
 ;; which loads jolt.crypto first and re-registers with RSA support — run
 ;; before any reference binds to the EC-only registrations.
 ;;
 ;; java.util.Base64 is deliberately absent: the runtime implements the
 ;; class, so a claim would be refused — getMimeDecoder is added at install.
 :jolt/provides {jolt.kmet.providers ["java.security.Signature"
                                      "java.security.KeyPairGenerator"
                                      "java.security.KeyFactory"
                                      "java.net.http.HttpTimeoutException"]}}
